f the first eigenvalue); default k ≈ 5–10 subject to spectral-gap inspection.\\n  - Projection: apply the fitted TICA map (mean-centered, with the same sin/cos basis) to the held-out frames of both distributions; otherwise the model never sees the test set.\\n\\n### (c) Distributional-similarity metrics per CV\\n- **1D marginals:** For each φ_i, ψ_i, ω_i, χ_j_i, and top-k TICA scores — KDE on a uniform grid (e.g., 50 bins for torsions, 36 for wrap-safe circular KDE; bandwidth by Silverman / Scott) and a periodic-aware weighted histogram. Metrics: Jensen–Shannon divergence (bits), 1-Wasserstein (circular W1 for torsions), both reported.\\n- **2D surfaces:** (φ_i, ψ_i) per residue (Ramachandran), (ψ_i, χ1_i), and (TICA-1, TICA-2). 2D histograms (e.g., 36×36), JSD on normalized counts.\\n- **Multivariate summary:** On top-k TICA components, energy distance or unbiased MMD with bootstrap CIs; optionally Wasserstein-2 after projecting onto the first two ICs (useful for free-energy-style plots F = −kT log p).\\n- **Effect-size calibration:** report JSD and W1 side by side with bootstrap 95% CIs (block bootstrap on replica-level blocks to respect autocorrelation).\\n\\n### (d) Controls and confounding safeguards\\n- Same featurization pipeline (single function, single version) for both sets; no per-set re-tuning.\\n- Same dt and temperature between ground-truth and (reported) sample-generation conditions; document any mismatch as a caveat rather than silently reweighting.\\n- TICA fit on ground-truth training split only — never on the generative samples — to avoid target leakage.\\n- Fixed integer seeds for KDE bandwidth selection, bootstrap resampling, and any random pair selection in the multivariate tests.\\n- Sample-size justification: report the effective sample size n_eff = n_frames·Δ/τ_int per distribution and a power note (JSD precision ≈ N_bins / n_eff for KDE-based metrics).\\n- Family-wise correction: Benjamini–Hochberg FDR across the family of CV-level tests, since we are running dozens of marginals.\\n\\n### (e) Feasibility inside this bubblewrap (CPU-only, no network, no preinstalled deps)\\n- **Hard constraint:** none of NumPy, SciPy, pandas, mdtraj, PyEMMA, scikit-learn, or matplotlib are importable; pip is not on PATH; network is off → cannot pip-install.\\n- **Consequence:** the experiment as designed above cannot be *executed* in this exact environment without vendored wheels or a prebuilt environment. The task document explicitly says \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\" and \\\"Do not claim that an experiment was run\\\", so the correct deliverable is the design — not a run. Where I do give example implementations in the full design, I will write them against a standard library (`pyemma.coordinates`, `mdtraj.compute_dihedrals`, `deeptime`) and call out that an actual execution needs those deps present.\\n- **CPU budget reality check (for sizing only):** on 24 CPUs, TICA on ≤ ~200k frames of (sin, cos)-encoded torsions (≈ 8–24 of them) is minutes-to-tens-of-minutes; KDE / 2D histograms on millions of frames is minutes. So the *design's compute footprint* is reasonable, even though the *current bubble* cannot host it.\\n\\n## 4) Assumptions (not supported by inspected inputs) — see `assumptions` field.\\n## 5) Open clarifications — see `uncertainties` field.\\n\\nStopping here per your instruction. No full design, no simulations, no fabricated runs. Awaiting your review/approval (and answers to the open clarifications in section 5 where possible) before I produce the complete experiment design in `stage=result`.\\n\",\"evidence\":\"Direct read of `/workspace/task.json` (the only file in the entire workspace): {\\\"question\\\":\\\"Do the sampled trajectories from the forward simulation model exhibit distributional similarity to ground-truth MD trajectories across key collective variables, such as backbone torsions, sidechain torsions, and top TICA components?\\\",\\\"context\\\":\\\"D