{"id": "98cdadc9-e53b-427a-abd2-e06417f22f5c", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:47:54.447931", "source": "user", "parent_id": "07bc4122-fa93-4ffa-babe-280dbdf6a67e", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach sketch is sound; resolving the consequential ambiguities now so the full design is unambiguous. Treat these as binding defaults (override only if the design text explicitly justifies the override): (1) Scope: design for a single canonical tetrapeptide as the worked example, but structure the protocol so any additional sequences can be added as additional panels without code changes. (2) Similarity target: equilibrium configurational p(x) is the primary endpoint; report per-frame-weighted autocorrelation times of TICA-1..TICA-k on each ensemble as a secondary kinetic sanity check, but do NOT make autocorrelation agreement a pass/fail criterion. (3) Sample weighting: assume unweighted configurations from both ground-truth MD and the forward model; if either source carries weights, the design must state reweighting rules but the headline metric stays JSD/W1 on empirical counts. (4) Library stack: specify mdtraj (DCD/XTC/PDB + topology; compute_phi/psi/omega/chi1; sin/cos basis as `compute_dihedral` or explicit ATOM selection) for featurization, PyEMMA or deeptime for TICA (lag in frames, k components, kinetic-map eigenvalue threshold), scipy.stats.entropy + scipy.stats.wassersteinstein_1d for JSD (natural log, bits) and 1-Wasserstein, scikit-learn or numpy KDE for circular-aware density estimation, matplotlib only for plot specifications. Include one short section titled 'Environment & Feasibility' that explicitly notes the present bubblewrap lacks these packages and has no network, so the design document itself ships as the deliverable; do not fabricate execution. (5) Featurization: phi_i, psi_i defined for all four residues of a linear tetrapeptide (terminal conventions documented explicitly, e.g. psi_N omitted or wrapped consistently); omega_i defined where the peptide bond is interior (between residues i and i+1, i=1..3); per-residue chi_j computed only for residues that actually have that chi (Gly/Pro have none). TICA input uses sin/cos-encoded torsions; per-torsion mean-centering is on training split only. (6) TICA hyperparameters: lag tau chosen by autocorrelation time of a fast backbone torsion (psi) on the training split, with a documented default ladder (try tau in {1,2,5,10,20} frames and report sensitivity); number of components k = min(10, rank of cumulant matrix; spectral-gap rule: include components whose implied timescale >= ceil(tau * reported_dt / autocorr_time_train); document the choice and the implied timescales. TICA is fit only on the ground-truth training-split. Projection of forward-model samples uses the fitted mean and tICA operator verbatim with no refitting. (7) Metrics: per-torsion 1D JSD (bits) and circular 1-Wasserstein on a 50-bin grid; per (phi_i,psi_i) Ramachandran, per (psi_i,chi1_i) where defined, and per (TICA-1,TICA-2) 2D JSD on a 36x36 grid; multivariate energy distance on the top 4 TICA components. Bootstrap 95% CIs by replica-level block bootstrap (block size chosen from training-set integrated autocorrelation time per CV). Benjamini-Hochberg FDR at q=0.05 across the per-CV family. Report an n_eff per distribution and a power note (precision floor ~ bins/n_eff for JSD). (8) Controls table in the design: matching (T, dt_save, equilibration prefix, termini capping, protonation, force field, water treatment), identical featurization function for both ensembles, no per-set KDE bandwidth retuning, fixed integer seeds, no TICA refit on test data. (9) Deliverable format: one Markdown design document with sections (Objectives, Distributions Compared, Featurization, TICA pipeline, Metrics, Controls, Statistical analysis plan, Compute & feasibility, Limitations); inline as code-fenced blocks: (i) CSV schema per CV row (CV_name, ensemble_size, n_eff, JSD_mean, JSD_CI_lo, JSD_CI_hi, W1_mean, W1_CI_lo, W1_CI_hi, p_value_MMD if applicable), (ii) figure manifests (Ramachandran per residue, psi vs chi1 where defined, TICA-1 vs TICA-2 free-energy overlay), (iii) pseudocode for the featurize / fit_tica / project / per-CV-metric / block-bootstrap / FDR pipeline. Produce the full design now. Do not execute code, do not fabricate numerical results beyond clearly-labeled illustrative placeholders, and do not regress to a second inspection — proceed straight to the complete deliverable document.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
{"id": "593fbce0-cea1-43fb-a55b-9f2cfaecb829", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:49:55.220715", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "98cdadc9-e53b-427a-abd2-e06417f22f5c", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "\n# Experiment Design — Distributional similarity of forward-model samples vs. ground-truth MD on a tetrapeptide\n\n**Deliverable type.** Design-only Markdown specification. No code is executed; numerical results below are **illustrative placeholders** explicitly flagged as such.\n\n**Binding defaults (per researcher):**\nendpoint = equilibrium configurational p(x); secondary output = per-frame-weighted TICA autocorrelation times (informational only, not pass/fail); configs are unweighted unless source declares weights (then reweighting rules below apply); library stack = mdtraj / PyEMMA or deeptime / scipy / scikit-learn / numpy / matplotlib; design ships as the deliverable because the bubblewrap is empty.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Objectives\n\n1. Quantify whether samples drawn from the forward simulation model reproduce the equilibrium backbone torsions (φ, ψ, ω), sidechain torsions (χ where defined), and top TICA components of a reference all-atom MD ensemble for a single canonical tetrapeptide.\n2. Report **per-CV** distributional similarity (JSD bits; circular 1-Wasserstein; 2D JSD on Ramachandran, ψ-vs-χ1, and TICA-1/TICA-2) with replica-level block-bootstrap 95% CIs.\n3. Report a **multivariate** summary on the top-4 TICA components (energy distance) and family-level decisions via Benjamini–Hochberg FDR at q = 0.05.\n4. Provide an **informational kinetic sanity** view (per-frame-weighted autocorrelation times of TICA-1..TICA-k on each ensemble) **without** using autocorrelation agreement as a pass/fail criterion.\n5. Be **panel-ready** so additional tetrapeptide sequences can be inserted with no protocol change — only dataset / topology changes.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Distributions compared\n\n### 2.1 Reference distribution — ground-truth MD\n- Source: all-atom MD trajectories of a single canonical tetrapeptide (worked example assumed to be e.g. Ace-Ala-Ala-Ala-Ala-NMe with one illustrative non-alanine variant used wherever χ atoms are needed to populate the sidechain-CVs protocol; the protocol does not depend on this choice).\n- State point: fixed temperature T and pressure P reported on both sources; force field, water treatment, protonation, termini capping recorded once and matched to the forward model's documented settings (see §7 Controls).\n- Equilibration prefix t_eq discarded per replica (e.g. first 1 ns of a 100 ns/frame × 100 replicas spec — values are **illustrative**); only decorrelated frames (per §4) enter scoring.\n- Replicas partitioned by **replica id** into training (fit) and evaluation (test) splits; ratios defaulted to 70% / 30% and fixed in `config.yaml` (see §6 Configuration).\n\n### 2.2 Sample distribution — forward model\n- Source: independent trajectories from the forward simulation model for the **same sequence**. The model is treated as a black box that emits (frame, optional weight).\n- Sample size: matches the ground-truth evaluation split in (a) per-replica length, (b) total number of replicas, (c) integration / saved-frame stride dt_save, and (d) reported thermodynamic conditions. Any mismatch is recorded in §7 but is **not** silently corrected by adjusting the protocol.\n\n### 2.3 Time-origin and split matching (binding)\n- Identical featurization function `featurize(traj)` is applied to every frame from both sources (§4).\n- TICA is fit only on the **ground-truth training-split** frames (§5). The forward-model frames are projected through the **already-fit** operator — no refit, no re-centering on test data.\n- For CIs, both ensembles are resampled independently by replica-level block bootstrap, preserving the within-block temporal correlation structure of each source.\n\n### 2.4 Weighting rules (binding secondary)\n- Default: unweighted configurations → empirical counts feed JSD / histograms / W1.\n- If a source declares per-frame weights w_i, the design enforces:\n  - Marginals become histograms with cell mass Σ w_i in each bin (PMFs p, q for JSD).\n  - For 2D JSD on joint surfaces, both 2D histograms are normalized using the same weighting so the KL terms are computed on weighted PMFs.\n  - For circular 1-Wasserstein, weights enter only the CDF step (weighted quantile computation); distances are reported in radians.\n  - For energy distance on top-k TICA, weights enter the energy statistic as a weighted U-statistic.\n- The headline metric on each CV remains **JSD / W1 on empirical (optionally weighted) counts**; no temperature-rescaling reweighting (Boltzmann correction) is performed unless specified by the researcher.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Featurization\n\n### 3.1 Library and atom indexing\n- `mdtraj` is used for trajectory I/O, topology handling, and dihedral computation.\n- Topology is loaded once per panel from the reference MD run; the same topology is reused for forward-model trajectories to guarantee identical atom naming.\n- All torsion definitions below use `mdtraj.compute_phi / compute_psi / compute_omega / compute_chi1 / compute_chi2 / compute_chi3 / compute_chi4`. When those helpers are unavailable due to topology quirks (e.g. some residue connect terms), fall back to explicit atom indices with `mdtraj.compute_dihedral`.\n\n### 3.2 Backbone torsions\nFor a linear tetrapeptide of 4 residues:\n\n| CV | Definition | Residue range |\n|---|---|---|\n| φ_i | C(i−1)–N(i)–CA(i)–C(i) | i = 1..4 (φ_1 defined using the N-terminal cap as the i−1 C-equivalent atom — recorded explicitly in the topology audit) |\n| ψ_i | N(i)–CA(i)–C(i)–N(i+1) | i = 1..4 (ψ_4 may be NaN in some conventions; we wrap it consistently by using the C-terminal cap as the i+1 N-equivalent atom and record this convention in `config.yaml`) |\n| ω_i | CA(i)–C(i)–N(i+1)–CA(i+1) | i = 1..3 (interior peptide bonds only) |\n\n**Terminal convention (binding):** both terminal φ_1 and ψ_4 are reported by substituting the cap atoms in the same position the next/previous residue would have occupied. This makes the 4 × 2 = 8 backbone pairs (φ_i, ψ_i) directly comparable between sources. The substitution atom names are recorded per panel and never changed mid-analysis.\n\n### 3.3 Sidechain torsions\n- χ_1-(i, j) is computed for **every residue where it is defined** per the recorded rotamer library:\n  - Gly, Pro (no χ_1): omitted from χ family; reported as `n_chi_defined[i] = 0` for that residue.\n  - Ala: n_chi_defined = 0.\n  - Other residues (Val, Leu, Ile, Ser, Thr, Cys, Met, Phe, Tyr, Trp, His, Asp, Asn, Glu, Gln, Lys, Arg): χ_1 always; higher χ included if defined.\n- For each defined χ, the design reports per-residue 1D JSD and (when χ_1 is defined together with ψ_i at the same residue) the 2D (ψ_i, χ_1_i) JSD.\n\n### 3.4 Periodic handling\n- All torsions wrapped to (−π, π] using `numpy.arctan2(sin, cos)` form.\n- For TICA input only, each torsion is encoded as two features (sin θ, cos θ).\n- For marginals and Ramachandran surfaces, raw angle values are used.\n\n---\n\n## 4. TICA pipeline\n\n### 4.1 Inputs and lag\n- Input features: `(sin, cos)`-encoded backbone φ, ψ (8 values), interior ω (3 residues × 2 = 6 values), and all defined χ (count from §3.3 × 2). Total feature dimension is panel-specific and reported in outputs.\n- Lag τ in **frames**. Default ladder {1, 2, 5, 10, 20}. τ is selected per panel as follows:\n  1. On the ground-truth-training frames, compute the integrated autocorrelation time τ_int of ψ of residue 1 as `1 + 2 Σ_{k=1..K} C(k)` with C(k) the autocorrelation up to the first negative value (cut at K where C(K) ≤ 0).\n  2. Anchor candidate τ values to fractions of τ_int: τ ∈ {1, 2, 5, 10, 20} frames intersected with ⌊τ_int / 4⌋, ⌊τ_int / 2⌋, ⌊τ_int⌋.\n  3. Run the full TICA pipeline at each candidate τ. The primary τ is the one whose **leading** implied timescale (see §4.3) is closest to but not exceeding τ_int (the slow-coordinates regime). Sensitivity (top-3 eigenvalues, JSD on top-2 IC coords, energy distance on top-4 ICs) is reported at every τ in the ladder.\n- Lag time τ is reported in **frames** and in **time** using dt_save from §7.\n\n### 4.2 Number of components\n- Compute eigenvalues λ_i of the tICA operator on the training split.\n- Implied timescale per component: t_i = − τ / ln(λ_i) for λ_i ∈ (0, 1).\n- Rank rule (binding):\n  - k_cap = min(10, rank(X) where X is the cumulative-covariance matrix of the TICA input features — i.e. ≤ number of non-zero-variance `(sin, cos)` tuples).\n  - Include component i iff λ_i ≥ 0.5 and t_i ≥ ⌈τ / τ_int_train⌉ × τ (in frames) / reported_dt — phrased in §4.3 as t_i ≥ τ_int_train (the autocorrelation time of the training split's fast variable).\n  - Report the spectral gap (Δ log λ_i − Δ log λ_{i+1} for the first 10 components) so the choice is auditable.\n\n### 4.3 Fit and project\n- Implementation:\n  - `pyemma.coordinates.tica(features_train, lag=tau, dim=k_cap, kinetic_map=True, mean=None)` — `mean=None` triggers `featurize` mean computation on the training split itself, then propagated to all subsequent calls.\n  - PyEMMA exposes `.transform(X)` for projection. Equivalent `deeptime.decomposition.TICA(lagtime=tau, dim=k_cap).fit(features_train).transform(features_test)` is documented as a swap-in alternative; the design picks PyEMMA and falls back to deeptime if PyEMMA is unavailable, with the resulting library recorded in `config.yaml`.\n- Project verbatim: the saved `meanzero`, the `mean` vector, the sine/cosine basis, the lag τ, and the projection matrices are applied to ground-truth-evaluation-split frames **and** to forward-model frames with **no refit, no re-centering**.\n\n### 4.4 Outputs\n- TICA scores `Z_train`, `Z_eval_MD`, `Z_Model` of shape `(n_frames, k_eff)` where k_eff is the number of components retained by §4.2.\n- Eigenvalues λ_1..λ_k_cap and implied timescales t_1..t_k_cap on the training split.\n- Spectral-gap table.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Metrics\n\n### 5.1 Per-CV metrics\n\n#### 5.1.1 1D marginals — JSD bits and circular 1-Wasserstein\nFor each torsion CV (φ_i, ψ_i, ω_i, χ_j_i where defined) **and** each TICA score (top k_eff):\n\n- Discretize to a 50-bin uniform grid on (−π, π] for torsions; on the empirical [min, max] range *of the pooled ground-truth+model training union* of TICA scores (to keep bins fixed across the two distributions). For TICA scores we use min-max range with a 2% extender on each end to prevent empty edge cells.\n- Bin masses p, q (probability mass per bin, summed to 1) with optional weights (per §2.4) and a small ε = 1e-12 added per cell before normalization to avoid log 0.\n- **Jensen–Shannon divergence (bits, natural log):**\n  ```\n  M = 0.5 * (p + q)\n  JSD(p, q) = 0.5 * (KL(p || M) + KL(q || M)) / ln(2)\n  ```\n  using `scipy.stats.entropy(pk, qk=None, base=None)` then dividing by `ln(2)` to render in bits.\n- **Circular 1-Wasserstein (radians):**\n  - Duplicate each sample with a shift of −2π and 2π, compute the weighted 1D Wasserstein on the tripled support window [−π − 2π, π + 2π], and take the minimum transport cost; closed form available via `scipy.stats.wasserstein_1d(u_vals, v_vals, v_weights=u_weights)` after sorting. The reported value is the **circular W1 in radians** (it is bounded by π).\n\n#### 5.1.2 2D surfaces\nPer residue (φ_i, ψ_i), per residue (ψ_i, χ_1_i) where both are defined, and on (TICA-1, TICA-2):\n\n- 36 × 36 grid; normalized counts (with optional weights per §2.4); ε smoothing as above.\n- JSD computed on the 1296-dimensional PMF via the same helper.\n\n#### 5.1.3 Multivariate (top-4 TICA components)\n- Energy distance (Székely–Rizzo) on the four-dimensional TICA score vectors. Implemented as `scipy.spatial.distance.energy_distance(u, Z_train_eval, ..., weights=..., ...)` or with explicit U-statistic. The reported number is the energy distance (units of TICA scores) plus a bootstrap p-value against the null of equal distributions using the same test statistic.\n\n### 5.2 Per-frame-weighted autocorrelation times (informational only)\nFor TICA-1..TICA-k_eff:\n- τ_int = 1 + 2 Σ_{k ≥ 1} C(k), cut at the first non-positive C(k).\n- C(k) is the per-frame-weighted TICA-score autocorrelation (sample-weighted, optional §2.4).\n- Reported **per ensemble** (ground-truth eval split and forward-model) in the CSV row's `aux_*` columns. **Not** used in any pass/fail decision.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Statistical analysis plan\n\n### 6.1 Effect size and CI\n- 95% CIs on JSD and W1 by replica-level block bootstrap:\n  - Block length ℓ_frames chosen per CV from the **training-split** autocorrelation time of that CV (so jittered coverage is in the units of \"blocks of training-typical correlation\").\n  - B = 200 bootstrap replicates (illustrative default; logged in config). For each replicate:\n    - Resample blocks with replacement within the **ground-truth eval split** → p*; within the **forward-model set** → q*; recompute JSD*(p*, q*) and W1*(p*, q*).\n  - CI = 2.5 / 97.5 percentiles of the bootstrap distribution.\n  - The same procedure applies for energy distance on top-4 ICs (with per-frame weighting only on the resampled frames).\n\n### 6.2 Family-wise error\n- Family = per-CV rows aggregated into two pre-defined buckets:\n  - Family A (1D torsion marginals): all defined φ_i, ψ_i, ω_i, χ_j_i. |A| pre-computed per panel.\n  - Family B (2D surfaces): Ramachandran per residue (4 rows), ψ-vs-χ1 per residue where defined, TICA-1×TICA-2. |B| pre-computed per panel.\n  - Family C (multivariate summaries): energy distance on top-4 TICA (one row).\n- Benjamini–Hochberg FDR at q = 0.05 within each family using `statsmodels.stats.multitest.multipletests(pvals, alpha=0.05, method='fdr_bh')`. JSD-based p_value is the bootstrap p-value (two-sided) for the hypothesis JSD ≤ JSD_threshold per CV; default threshold = JSD ^ floor of bins/n_eff_actionability (see §6.3 power note). Family A and B use JSD-derived p-values; Family C uses the energy-distance p-value.\n\n### 6.3 n_eff and power\n- Effective sample size n_eff per distribution = n_frames · dt_save / τ_int (training-split τ_int of the cached \"fast variable\" ψ residue 1).\n- JSD precision floor ≈ bins / n_eff. Reported per CV in CSV `aux_*` columns: `(n_eff_MD, n_eff_Model, precision_floor_JSD_bits)`.\n- The power note: with n_eff per side, the minimum detectable JSD (effect size) at power 0.8 for a BS distribution with B = 200 is reported as `JSD_detectable ≈ 2 × precision_floor + 0.005 bits` (rule-of-thumb; logged for reproducibility).\n\n### 6.4 Seeds and determinism\n- All randomness (block-bootstrap indices, KDE bandwiths if KDE is used in place of histograms) uses a single `SEED` integer from `config.yaml`. Default `SEED = 0xC0FFEE` (illustrative; replace per project).\n- No per-set re-tuning of KDE bandwidths, bin edges, or tICA hyperparameters.\n\n### 6.5 Failure modes handled explicitly\n- If both populations have all-zero variance on a CV (e.g. ω staying near 180°), JSD over (ε, 0, ε, …) is reported as NaN with reason `low_variance`.\n- If a residue has no defined torsion (Gly / Pro / Ala), that CV row is omitted from family A pseudocode at run time and BH FDR proceeds over the remaining rows.\n\n### 6.6 Configuration schema (binding shape)\n\n```yaml\n# config.yaml — single panel; replicate key 'panels' for additional sequences\npanel_id: \"ala4_ace_nme\"                      # illustrative name\ntopology: \"/abs/path/to/ref.pdb\"\nmd_trajectories:\n  format: \"dcd\"                              # or xtc, nc, h5\n  files:   [ \"/abs/path/md/replica_*.dcd\" ]   # exactly N replicas\n  dt_save_fs: 100                            # used both for frame-to-time and tau\n  t_equil_ns: 1.0                             # discarded at start per replica\n  replicas: 100\nforward_model:\n  source: \"model://forward_v1\"\n  trajectories:\n    format: \"dcd\"\n    files:   [ \"/abs/path/model/replica_*.dcd\" ]\n  reported_T_K: 300\n  reported_dt_save_fs: 100\nsplitting:\n  train_fraction: 0.7                         # by replica id\n  seed: 12648430\ntica:\n  lag_ladder_frames: [1, 2, 5, 10, 20]\n  selection_rule: \"first_tau_with_leading_timescale_le_tau_int_train\"\n  library: \"pyemma\"                          # or \"deeptime\"\n  max_components: 10\n  kinetic_map: true\nmetrics:\n  bins_1d: 50\n  bins_2d: 36\n  epsilon: 1.0e-12\nbootstrap:\n  n_replicates: 200\n  block_strategy: \"from_tau_int_train_per_cv\"\n  seed: 12648430\nfdr:\n  q: 0.05\n  family_definition:\n    A: \"torsions_1d\"\n    B: \"surfaces_2d\"\n    C: \"energy_distance_top4\"\noutput:\n  csv: \"/abs/path/results/per_cv.csv\"\n  figure_dir: \"/abs/path/figures/\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Controls\n\n| Control | Description | Verified by |\n|---|---|---|\n| `T` matched | Ground-truth reported temperature = forward-model reported temperature | unit test on `config.yaml` + assertion `md.reported_T_K == forward_model.reported_T_K` |\n| `dt_save` matched | Frame-to-time conversion same on both sides | `assert md.dt_save_fs == forward_model.reported_dt_save_fs` |\n| `t_equil` matched | Equilibration prefix discarded on both sides | log printed per panel; if forward model does not declare t_equil, design flags `no_t_equil_declared: true` and reports results both with and without discarding the first 10% of frames as a sensitivity row |\n| `termini capping, protonation, force field, water treatment` matched | Topology source = single source of truth; topology file used by featurize is the one shipped with the reference MD run; topology audit table records every substitution atom in `config.yaml`'s `termini` block | diff at run start: `featurize(MD_frame_0) == featurize(Model_frame_0)` upto index ordering |\n| identical featurization function | One `featurize()` implementation is reused for both ensembles; no per-source code path | grep for `featurize(` call sites limited to `featurize/` module |\n| no per-set KDE bandwidth | Either histogram-only (bins fixed by §6) or KDE bandwidth fixed to a single Silverman value computed on the pooled training-split TICA scores | unit test asserts no per-set bandwidth override |\n| fixed integer seeds | All randomness uses `SEED` from `config.yaml`; no `np.random.seed` inside loops | grep + assertion |\n| no TICA refit on test data | Fit happens once on training split; `.transform()` only calls | assertion that `tica.fit` is called at most once and `tica.transform` does not mutate state |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Compute & feasibility\n\n### 8.1 Environment\n- The current bubblewrap at `/workspace` contains only `/workspace/task.json`. `python3` is present at `/usr/bin/python3`. Numeric packages (NumPy, SciPy, pandas, mdtraj, PyEMMA, deeptime, scikit-learn, matplotlib) are **not** importable. The pip executable is not on `PATH`. Network access is disabled. Consequently **no Python execution of the analysis is performed here**; this document is the deliverable.\n- The design assumes the eventual runtime is a CPU-only Linux with the following versions (illustrative; logged in `requirements.txt`):\n  - Python 3.10+\n  - `mdtraj >= 1.9`, `pyemma >= 2.5` (or `deeptime >= 0.4`), `scipy >= 1.10`, `scikit-learn >= 1.2`, `numpy >= 1.23`, `pandas >= 1.5`, `matplotlib >= 3.6`.\n\n### 8.2 Compute sizing (binding)\n- Featurization (`compute_phi/psi/omega/chi1..n` × 4 residues, max ~100k frames per replica, ~100 replicas): on 24 CPUs, low single-digit minutes.\n- Mean + covariance + lagged covariance + eigendecomposition for the tICA input features: tens of seconds per τ on the chosen training split.\n- KDE / 1D and 2D histograms: seconds per CV.\n- Block bootstrap (B = 200) over the eval split on the largest CV family: low single-digit minutes; dominates wall-clock.\n- Total wall-clock for a single panel: **under one hour of CPU time** at the illustrative panel size. Memory peak ≤ ~2 GB numpy arrays for the largest expected training split (illustrative).\n\n### 8.3 Network and install policy inside the bubble\n- The protocol presupposes `pip install` for the libraries above. This is **not** available in the present bubble. The Markdown design is therefore shipped as the final deliverable; no execution artifact is produced.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Limitations (stated explicitly)\n\n- The design is single-panel by default. Multi-panel execution reuses all of §3–§6 without code changes but multiplies compute; CI values are computed **per panel** and not pooled.\n- \"Distributional similarity\" is the equilibrium p(x) picture. Kinetics are **not** graded for pass/fail; the autocorrelation row is informational.\n- If the forward model does not declare weights, weights are uniformly 1.\n- If MD and model use different integration `dt`, the protocol does not rescale; we record `dt_save` as a control and report sensitivity only if the mismatch is small (a separate standalone check described in §6.6; not used for pass/fail).\n- JSD floors at 0 and W1 floors at 0 radians; both are documented lower bounds that depend on KDE/histogram resolution. Power floor is reported per CV.\n- The reference backbone-dihedral computation in mdtraj assigns φ_1 and ψ_4 by using cap atoms; this convention is non-universal and is recorded per panel. Re-running with a different cap convention will change ψ_4 / φ_1 by an atom-shift of one bond, which can slightly alter JSD on those two CVs only.\n- If MD and model samples are tiny (n_frames per replica < 100), bootstrap variance dominates; the design flags `n_eff_low: true` and declines to compute BH FDR on Family C (energy distance) only — Families A and B continue with the appropriate JSD floor caveat.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Inline deliverables\n\n### 10.1 Per-CV CSV row schema (binding)\n\n```\nCV_name, ensemble_size, n_eff,\nJSD_mean, JSD_CI_lo, JSD_CI_hi,\nW1_mean, W1_CI_lo, W1_CI_hi,\np_value_MMD_if_applicable,\naux_var_MD, aux_var_Model, aux_tau_int_MD, aux_tau_int_Model,\nfamily_tag,\npanel_id\n```\n\n| Field | Type | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| `CV_name` | str | e.g. `phi_2`, `psi_1`, `omega_2`, `chi1_3`, `TICA_1`, `ram_2`, `psichi1_3`, `tica12` |\n| `ensemble_size` | int | number of frames in the eval split (smaller of MD eval vs. Model total) |\n| `n_eff` | float | min(n_eff_MD, n_eff_Model) per §6.3 |\n| `JSD_mean` | float (bits) | point estimate JSD on full eval splits |\n| `JSD_CI_lo / _hi` | float | 2.5 / 97.5 percentile of B block-bootstrap replicates |\n| `W1_mean`, `W1_CI_lo`, `W1_CI_hi` | float (radians for torsions, TICA-units otherwise) | circular W1 on torsions (else linear W1 over the bin range) |\n| `p_value_MMD_if_applicable` | float or null | energy-distance bootstrap p-value for Family C only; null for Families A, B |\n| `aux_var_MD`, `aux_var_Model` | float | sample variances of the CV after per-CV discretization |\n| `aux_tau_int_MD`, `aux_tau_int_Model` | float (frames) | per-frame-weighted τ_int of the CV (informational) |\n| `family_tag` | enum | `A_torsions_1d`, `B_surfaces_2d`, `C_energy_distance` |\n| `panel_id` | str | mirrors `config.yaml`'s top-level key |\n\n### 10.2 Figure manifests (binding)\n\nEach figure is a PNG written to `output.figure_dir/<panel_id>/<figure_name>.png` at 150 dpi.\n\n| ID | Filename | Description | Type | Library |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | `rama_<resi>.png` | Ramachandran (φ, ψ) per residue; ground-truth and model contours overlaid; free-energy levels at 1, 2, 4, 8 kT | 2D contour overlay | matplotlib |\n| F2 | `psi_chi1_<resi>.png` | 2D (ψ, χ_1) per residue where χ_1 defined | 2D contour overlay | matplotlib |\n| F3 | `tica12_overlay.png` | 2D (TICA-1, TICA-2) free-energy overlay, ground truth vs. model | 2D contour overlay | matplotlib |\n| F4 | `tica_scree_<panel>.png` | tICA eigenvalues and implied timescales vs. component index | 1D line + twin axis | matplotlib |\n| F5 | `tau_sensitivity_<panel>.png` | Top-3 metrics (leading eigenvalue, JSD on TICA-1/2, energy distance on top-4 ICs) vs. τ in lag ladder | 1D line | matplotlib |\n| F6 | `cv_jitter_<panel>.png` | Mean JSD per CV family with 95% bars | dot-and-whisker | matplotlib |\n| F7 | `autocorr_times_<panel>.png` | τ_int per TICA component on MD and Model (informational) | 1D line | matplotlib |\n\n### 10.3 Pseudocode (binding)\n\nAll pseudocode in fenced blocks below is descriptive; no execution is undertaken.\n\n#### 10.3.1 `featurize`\n\n```python\n# featurize/torsions.py\nimport numpy as np\nimport mdtraj as md\n\ndef residues_with_chi(topo, j):\n    \"\"\"Return list of residue indices whose sidechain exposes chi_j.\"\"\"\n    # For j=1 use md.compute_chi1 indices; skip residues with NaN definition.\n    return [r.index for r in topo.residues if has_chi(r, j)]\n\ndef featurize(traj: md.Trajectory) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compute, for a single Trajectory, the per-residue torsion arrays\n    plus their (sin, cos) encodings.\n    Returns a dict with keys: 'phi' (n,4), 'psi' (n,4), 'omega' (n,3),\n    'chi_j' for each defined j, 'chi_indices', and the sin/cos blocks.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = traj.n_frames\n    out = {\n        'phi': md.compute_phi(traj)[1],                 # shape (n, 4)\n        'psi': md.compute_psi(traj)[1],                 # shape (n, 4)\n        'omega': md.compute_omega(traj)[1],             # shape (n, 3)\n        'chi_indices': {}\n    }\n    for j in range(1, 5):\n        idx = residues_with_chi(traj.topology, j)\n        if not idx:\n            continue\n        chi_vals, chooser = md.compute_chi(j)(traj)     # shape (n, len(idx))\n        out[f'chi{j}'] = chi_vals\n        out['chi_indices'][f'chi{j}'] = idx\n\n    # Periodic encoding for TICA input only.\n    def enc(arr):\n        # Wrap to (-pi, pi] defensively (mdtraj already does this).\n        return np.stack([np.sin(arr), np.cos(arr)], axis=-1)  # (n, k, 2)\n    tica_blocks = []\n    for key in ['phi', 'psi', 'omega']:\n        tica_blocks.append(enc(out[key]).reshape(n, -1))      # (n, k*2)\n    for j in range(1, 5):\n        if f'chi{j}' in out:\n            tica_blocks.append(enc(out[f'chi{j}']).reshape(n, -1))\n    out['tica_input'] = np.concatenate(tica_blocks, axis=1)\n    return out\n```\n\n#### 10.3.2 `fit_tica`\n\n```python\n# tica/fit.py\nimport numpy as np, pyemma.coordinates as pc\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.stattools import acf\n# (deeptime alternative path documented but not executed in pseudocode)\n\ndef estimate_tau_int_train(features_train_1d, dt_save_fs):\n    \"\"\"Integrated autocorrelation time of psi_1 (uses column index from\n    earlier featurize call). Cuts at first non-positive autocorrelation.\"\"\"\n    series = features_train_1d[:, psi1_column]\n    c = acf(series, nlags=len(series)//2, fft=True)\n    tau = 1.0\n    for k in range(1, len(c)):\n        if c[k] <= 0:\n            break\n        tau += 2.0 * c[k]\n    return tau\n\ndef fit_tica(traj_md_train, tau, k_cap):\n    \"\"\"One fit, on the ground-truth training split only.\"\"\"\n    feats = featurize(traj_md_train)['tica_input']      # (n, d)\n    tica = pc.tica(data=feats, lag=tau, dim=k_cap,\n                   kinetic_map=True, mean=None)         # mean=None -> learned\n    return tica\n\ndef project(tica, feats):\n    \"\"\"Apply the fitted tICA operator verbatim.\"\"\"\n    return tica.transform(feats)\n```\n\n#### 10.3.3 `per_cv_metric`\n\n```python\n# metrics/per_cv.py\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import entropy, wasserstein_1d\n\ndef jsd_bits(p, q, eps=1e-12):\n    p = (np.asarray(p) + eps); q = (np.asarray(q) + eps)\n    p = p / p.sum(); q = q / q.sum()\n    m = 0.5 * (p + q)\n    return (entropy(p, m, base=None) + entropy(q, m, base=None)) / np.log(2)\n\ndef circular_wasserstein_1d(u_vals, v_vals, u_weights=None, v_weights=None):\n    \"\"\"Circular 1-Wasserstein via triplication on [-3pi, 3pi]. Returns radians.\"\"\"\n    def shift(arr, w, delta):\n        return np.concatenate([arr + delta for _ in (1,)])  # placeholder\n    # Pseudocode; concrete impl: triple concatenate with shifts -2pi, 0, +2pi,\n    # run weighted wasserstein_1d on the doubled supports, return minimum / 2.\n    triples_u = np.concatenate([u_vals - 2*np.pi, u_vals, u_vals + 2*np.pi])\n    triples_v = np.concatenate([v_vals - 2*np.pi, v_vals, v_vals + 2*np.pi])\n    wU = np.tile(u_weights, 3) if u_weights is not None else None\n    wV = np.tile(v_weights, 3) if v_weights is not None else None\n    return wasserstein_1d(triples_u, triples_v, v_weights=wU, u_weights=wV) / 3.0\n\ndef per_cv_metric(p_hist, q_hist, edges, two_d=False):\n    \"\"\"Args: 1D or 2D PMFs over the same fixed grid; returns dict.\"\"\"\n    return {\n        'JSD_mean': jsd_bits(p_hist, q_hist),\n        # For 1D torsion CVs, also compute circular W1:\n        #   W1 = circular_wasserstein_1d(samples_md, samples_model, ...)\n        # and store under W1_mean.\n        # W1 reported only for 1D torsion rows, so two_d=False branch.\n    }\n```\n\n#### 10.3.4 Block bootstrap\n\n```python\n# stats/bootstrap.py\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef block_length_from_tau_int(series, dt_save_fs):\n    \"\"\"Block length in frames for replica-level bootstrap on a single CV.\"\"\"\n    c = autocorrelation(series)\n    tau = 1 + 2 * c_positive_sum(c)\n    return max(1, int(np.ceil(tau)))\n\ndef block_bootstrap(md_eval_features_by_replica,\n                    model_features_by_replica,\n                    metric_fn, block_len_md, block_len_model,\n                    B=200, seed=12648430):\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    boot = []\n    for _ in range(B):\n        md_blocks = sample_blocks(md_eval_features_by_replica, block_len_md, rng)\n        mod_blocks = sample_blocks(model_features_by_replica, block_len_model, rng)\n        boot.append(metric_fn(np.concatenate(md_blocks),\n                              np.concatenate(mod_blocks)))\n    return np.percentile(boot, [2.5, 97.5]), boot\n```\n\n#### 10.3.5 FDR application\n\n```python\n# stats/fdr.py\nfrom statsmodels.stats.multitest import multipletests\n\ndef decide_families(per_cv_rows, q=0.05):\n    out = {}\n    for fam in ['A_torsions_1d', 'B_surfaces_2d', 'C_energy_distance']:\n        rows = [r for r in per_cv_rows if r['family_tag'] == fam]\n        if fam == 'C_energy_distance':\n            pvals = [r['p_value_MMD_if_applicable'] for r in rows]\n        else:\n            pvals = [2 * (1 - cdf_of_jsd(r)) for r in rows]\n        reject, p_adj, _, _ = multipletests(pvals, alpha=q, method='fdr_bh')\n        for r, rej, pa in zip(rows, reject, p_adj):\n            r['reject_null'] = bool(rej)\n            r['p_adj'] = float(pa)\n        out[fam] = rows\n    return out\n```\n\n#### 10.3.6 CSV emission\n\n```python\n# io/csv_emitter.py\nimport csv\n\nFIELDNAMES = [\n    'CV_name', 'ensemble_size', 'n_eff',\n    'JSD_mean', 'JSD_CI_lo', 'JSD_CI_hi',\n    'W1_mean', 'W1_CI_lo', 'W1_CI_hi',\n    'p_value_MMD_if_applicable',\n    'aux_var_MD', 'aux_var_Model',\n    'aux_tau_int_MD', 'aux_tau_int_Model',\n    'family_tag', 'panel_id'\n]\n\ndef emit(rows, path):\n    with open(path, 'w', newline='') as f:\n        w = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=FIELDNAMES)\n        w.writeheader()\n        for r in rows:\n            w.writerow({k: r.get(k, '') for k in FIELDNAMES})\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11. Illustrative output (placeholders only — no run performed)\n\nA representative row of `per_cv.csv` after running on the worked-example panel (numbers are placeholders):\n\n```\nCV_name,ensemble_size,n_eff,JSD_mean,JSD_CI_lo,JSD_CI_hi,W1_mean,W1_CI_lo,W1_CI_hi,p_value_MMD_if_applicable,aux_var_MD,aux_var_Model,aux_tau_int_MD,aux_tau_int_Model,family_tag,panel_id\nphi_2,210000,7943.5,0.0031,0.0028,0.0035,0.041,0.038,0.044,,0.91,0.90,2.4,2.5,A_torsions_1d,ala4_ace_nme\npsi_2,210000,7860.1,0.0029,0.0026,0.0033,0.038,0.036,0.041,,0.88,0.88,2.5,2.6,A_torsions_1d,ala4_ace_nme\nomega_2,210000,8200.0,0.0012,0.0010,0.0014,,      ,,0.05,0.05,3.0,3.1,A_torsions_1d,ala4_ace_nme\nchi1_3,210000,7000.0,0.0201,0.0185,0.0221,0.151,0.143,0.161,,1.10,1.21,3.4,3.8,A_torsions_1d,ala4_ace_nme\nram_2,210000,7943.5,0.0064,0.0058,0.0070,,,,,0.85,0.83,,,B_surfaces_2d,ala4_ace_nme\npsichi1_3,210000,7000.0,0.0312,0.0294,0.0334,,,,,0.92,0.95,,,B_surfaces_2d,ala4_ace_nme\ntica12,210000,7943.5,0.0044,0.0040,0.0048,,,,,1.05,1.05,,,B_surfaces_2d,ala4_ace_nme\nplanar_top4,210000,7943.5,,,, ,,,,1.0,0.012,0.005,0.022,,,,,C_energy_distance,ala4_ace_nme\n```\n*(all numeric values above are illustrative placeholders)*\n\nA representative set of 7 figure files (paths under `output.figure_dir/ala4_ace_nme/`):\n- `rama_1.png`, `rama_2.png`, `rama_3.png`, `rama_4.png`\n- `psi_chi1_2.png`, `psi_chi1_3.png` (only residues where χ_1 is defined)\n- `tica12_overlay.png`\n- `tica_scree.png`\n- `tau_sensitivity.png`\n- `cv_jitter.png`\n- `autocorr_times.png`\n\nA representative FDR outcome (illustrative):\n- Family A (`A_torsions_1d`): per-CV JSD-derived p-values, BH-adjusted; in this placeholder run, all rows show `reject_null = False` (preserved_null) due to small effect sizes with tight CIs, except `chi1_3` which is flagged preserved_null but with JSD_mean = 0.020 (above the bins/n_eff floor of 0.0071) — represented here as informational, not as a pass/fail statement.\n- Family B (`B_surfaces_2d`): analogous.\n- Family C (`C_energy_distance`): `planar_top4` energy-distance bootstrap p-value placeholder = 0.012; BH-adjusted at the family-level (single row test) preserves rejection control at q = 0.05.\n\n*(All values illustrative; not from any actual run.)*\n\n---\n\n## 12. Stated assumptions and open clarifications — resolved status\n\nThe original plan's open items 1–12 are **resolved by the binding defaults** above:\n1. Sequence/scope → single canonical panel, multi-panel-ready (§6.6).\n2. Endpoint → equilibrium p(x); kinetic is informational only (§1, §5.2).\n3. Weights → unweighted default; rules in §2.4 if present.\n4. Library stack → §8.1 records it; §10.3 pseudocode targets it.\n5. Lag & k → §4.1 ladder + §4.2 spectral-gap rule.\n6. Featurization caps → §3.2 bound to cap substitution; recorded in config.\n7. JSD bits → §5.1.1.\n8. Block-bootstrap · FDR · n_eff · power note → §6.1–§6.3.\n9. Controls → §7 table binding.\n10. Deliverable format → §10.\n11. ε smoothing → §5.1.1 (`epsilon = 1e-12`).\n12. Informational kinematic check (no pass/fail) → §5.2.\n\nItems that are still upstream of the design (i.e. data not in the repo) are:\n- exact identity of the canonical tetrapeptide, force field, water treatment;\n- exact format / path of MD and forward-model trajectories;\n- exact vers of mdtraj / PyEMMA / deeptime / scipy / scikit-learn / matplotlib;\n- whether a future revision wants temperature-rescaling reweighting.\n\nThese are intentionally left to the project owner; the protocol is unchanged by the answers and §6.6 captures their pinned values.\n\n---\n\n**End of design.** Document is the deliverable; no execution was performed.\n", "evidence": "Initial inspection evidence (verbatim): `/workspace/task.json` is the only file in the repo. Its `question` reads \"Do the sampled trajectories from the forward simulation model exhibit distributional similarity to ground-truth MD trajectories across key collective variables, such as backbone torsions, sidechain torsions, and top TICA components?\". Its `context` reads \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\" Its `documents` map is `{\"track.md\": \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"}` as an inline string with no on-disk counterpart. `find /workspace -type f` returns only `/workspace/task.json`. `ls /workspace/.*` returns no hidden files. `find / -maxdepth 4 -type f \\( -name 'track.md' -o -name '*tetrapeptide*' -o -name '*alanine*' -o -name '*neurips2024*' \\)` returns no project-relevant matches. `importlib.util.find_spec` confirms numpy/scipy/pandas/torch/mdtraj/pyemma/sklearn/matplotlib are MISSING; `which` not on PATH; `env` shows only `PWD=/workspace`; Python 3.12.3 at /usr/bin/python3; no pip / no conda. The full design ships as the deliverable per the binding defaults ratified by the researcher.", "assumptions": ["Single canonical tetrapeptide panel (with multi-panel extensibility baked into config schema and pipeline).", "The forward model emits unweighted coordinates unless per-frame weights are declared.", "Ground-truth MD and forward-model share (T, P, dt_save, force field, water treatment, protonation, termini capping); any deviation is logged in §7 as a flagged control rather than silently corrected.", "TICA lag tau is chosen from the documented lattice {1,2,5,10,20} anchored by psi_1 integrated autocorrelation time; number of components k is dictated by the spectral-gap rule (eigenvalue >= 0.5 and implied timescale >= tau_int_train) capped at 10.", "JSD computed in bits (natural log, divided by ln(2)) on a 50-bin 1D grid; 2D JSD on 36x36 grids; circular 1-Wasserstein in radians for torsion CVs only.", "Bh FDR is applied independently within three pre-defined families (A_torsions_1d, B_surfaces_2d, C_energy_distance), at q = 0.05, using statsmodels multipletests.", "Informational TICA autocorrelation times (per-frame-weighted) are reported per ensemble and never used as a pass/fail criterion.", "Plus-args versions (mdtraj >= 1.9, pyemma >= 2.5 or deeptime >= 0.4, scipy >= 1.10, scikit-learn >= 1.2, etc.) are illustrative; actual versions are pinned in requirements.txt at run time.", "The present bubblewrap cannot install or import these libraries; this Markdown document is the deliverable and no execution is performed."], "uncertainties": ["Exact identity of the canonical tetrapeptide sequence, force field, water treatment are not source-supported in the repo; protocol does not depend on these choices per the binding defaults (panel-config-driven).", "Exact file format / path / version history of MD and forward-model trajectories are not in the repo; logged in config.yaml at run time.", "Whether temperature-rescaling / Boltzmann reweighting should be applied if MD and Model differ in reported T is not pre-decided; design currently logs the mismatch as a flagged control without applying any reweighting (binding §2.4).", "Whether the canonical panel is actually Ace-Ala4-NMe or a different sequence with chi-bearing residues is open; the protocol works regardless.", "Whether to use PyEMMA or deeptime at run time depends on which is available; both are supported and the choice is recorded in config.yaml.", "Future tweaks to the lag ladder, FDR q, or block-bootstrap replicate count B can be made by editing config.yaml without changing the protocol."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-93c5e1a6-387b-4f03-965b-1be5b7db58e8", "tool_call": {"id": "call-93c5e1a6-387b-4f03-965b-1be5b7db58e8", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Full experiment design for MD vs forward-model distributional similarity on tetrapeptide CVs\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"\\n# Experiment Design \\u2014 Distributional similarity of forward-model samples vs. ground-truth MD on a tetrapeptide\\n\\n**Deliverable type.** Design-only Markdown specification. No code is executed; numerical results below are **illustrative placeholders** explicitly flagged as such.\\n\\n**Binding defaults (per researcher):**\\nendpoint = equilibrium configurational p(x); secondary output = per-frame-weighted TICA autocorrelation times (informational only, not pass/fail); configs are unweighted unless source declares weights (then reweighting rules below apply); library stack = mdtraj / PyEMMA or deeptime / scipy / scikit-learn / numpy / matplotlib; design ships as the deliverable because the bubblewrap is empty.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Objectives\\n\\n1. Quantify whether samples drawn from the forward simulation model reproduce the equilibrium backbone torsions (\\u03c6, \\u03c8, \\u03c9), sidechain torsions (\\u03c7 where defined), and top TICA components of a reference all-atom MD ensemble for a single canonical tetrapeptide.\\n2. Report **per-CV** distributional similarity (JSD bits; circular 1-Wasserstein; 2D JSD on Ramachandran, \\u03c8-vs-\\u03c71, and TICA-1/TICA-2) with replica-level block-bootstrap 95% CIs.\\n3. Report a **multivariate** summary on the top-4 TICA components (energy distance) and family-level decisions via Benjamini\\u2013Hochberg FDR at q = 0.05.\\n4. Provide an **informational kinetic sanity** view (per-frame-weighted autocorrelation times of TICA-1..TICA-k on each ensemble) **without** using autocorrelation agreement as a pass/fail criterion.\\n5. Be **panel-ready** so additional tetrapeptide sequences can be inserted with no protocol change \\u2014 only dataset / topology changes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Distributions compared\\n\\n### 2.1 Reference distribution \\u2014 ground-truth MD\\n- Source: all-atom MD trajectories of a single canonical tetrapeptide (worked example assumed to be e.g. Ace-Ala-Ala-Ala-Ala-NMe with one illustrative non-alanine variant used wherever \\u03c7 atoms are needed to populate the sidechain-CVs protocol; the protocol does not depend on this choice).\\n- State point: fixed temperature T and pressure P reported on both sources; force field, water treatment, protonation, termini capping recorded once and matched to the forward model's documented settings (see \\u00a77 Controls).\\n- Equilibration prefix t_eq discarded per replica (e.g. first 1 ns of a 100 ns/frame \\u00d7 100 replicas spec \\u2014 values are **illustrative**); only decorrelated frames (per \\u00a74) enter scoring.\\n- Replicas partitioned by **replica id** into training (fit) and evaluation (test) splits; ratios defaulted to 70% / 30% and fixed in `config.yaml` (see \\u00a76 Configuration).\\n\\n### 2.2 Sample distribution \\u2014 forward model\\n- Source: independent trajectories from the forward simulation model for the **same sequence**. The model is treated as a black box that emits (frame, optional weight).\\n- Sample size: matches the ground-truth evaluation split in (a) per-replica length, (b) total number of replicas, (c) integration / saved-frame stride dt_save, and (d) reported thermodynamic conditions. Any mismatch is recorded in \\u00a77 but is **not** silently corrected by adjusting the protocol.\\n\\n### 2.3 Time-origin and split matching (binding)\\n- Identical featurization function `featurize(traj)` is applied to every frame from both sources (\\u00a74).\\n- TICA is fit only on the **ground-truth training-split** frames (\\u00a75). The forward-model frames are projected through the **already-fit** operator \\u2014 no refit, no re-centering on test data.\\n- For CIs, both ensembles are resampled independently by replica-level block bootstrap, preserving the within-block temporal correlation structure of each source.\\n\\n### 2.4 Weighting rules (binding secondary)\\n- Default: unweighted configurations \\u2192 empirical counts feed JSD / histograms / W1.\\n- If a source declares per-frame weights w_i, the design enforces:\\n  - Marginals become histograms with cell mass \\u03a3 w_i in each bin (PMFs p, q for JSD).\\n  - For 2D JSD on joint surfaces, both 2D histograms are normalized using the same weighting so the KL terms are computed on weighted PMFs.\\n  - For circular 1-Wasserstein, weights enter only the CDF step (weighted quantile computation); distances are reported in radians.\\n  - For energy distance on top-k TICA, weights enter the energy statistic as a weighted U-statistic.\\n- The headline metric on each CV remains **JSD / W1 on empirical (optionally weighted) counts**; no temperature-rescaling reweighting (Boltzmann correction) is performed unless specified by the researcher.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Featurization\\n\\n### 3.1 Library and atom indexing\\n- `mdtraj` is used for trajectory I/O, topology handling, and dihedral computation.\\n- Topology is loaded once per panel from the reference MD run; the same topology is reused for forward-model trajectories to guarantee identical atom naming.\\n- All torsion definitions below use `mdtraj.compute_phi / compute_psi / compute_omega / compute_chi1 / compute_chi2 / compute_chi3 / compute_chi4`. When those helpers are unavailable due to topology quirks (e.g. some residue connect terms), fall back to explicit atom indices with `mdtraj.compute_dihedral`.\\n\\n### 3.2 Backbone torsions\\nFor a linear tetrapeptide of 4 residues:\\n\\n| CV | Definition | Residue range |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| \\u03c6_i | C(i\\u22121)\\u2013N(i)\\u2013CA(i)\\u2013C(i) | i = 1..4 (\\u03c6_1 defined using the N-terminal cap as the i\\u22121 C-equivalent atom \\u2014 recorded explicitly in the topology audit) |\\n| \\u03c8_i | N(i)\\u2013CA(i)\\u2013C(i)\\u2013N(i+1) | i = 1..4 (\\u03c8_4 may be NaN in some conventions; we wrap it consistently by using the C-terminal cap as the i+1 N-equivalent atom and record this convention in `config.yaml`) |\\n| \\u03c9_i | CA(i)\\u2013C(i)\\u2013N(i+1)\\u2013CA(i+1) | i = 1..3 (interior peptide bonds only) |\\n\\n**Terminal convention (binding):** both terminal \\u03c6_1 and \\u03c8_4 are reported by substituting the cap atoms in the same position the next/previous residue would have occupied. This makes the 4 \\u00d7 2 = 8 backbone pairs (\\u03c6_i, \\u03c8_i) directly comparable between sources. The substitution atom names are recorded per panel and never changed mid-analysis.\\n\\n### 3.3 Sidechain torsions\\n- \\u03c7_1-(i, j) is computed for **every residue where it is defined** per the recorded rotamer library:\\n  - Gly, Pro (no \\u03c7_1): omitted from \\u03c7 family; reported as `n_chi_defined[i] = 0` for that residue.\\n  - Ala: n_chi_defined = 0.\\n  - Other residues (Val, Leu, Ile, Ser, Thr, Cys, Met, Phe, Tyr, Trp, His, Asp, Asn, Glu, Gln, Lys, Arg): \\u03c7_1 always; higher \\u03c7 included if defined.\\n- For each defined \\u03c7, the design reports per-residue 1D JSD and (when \\u03c7_1 is defined together with \\u03c8_i at the same residue) the 2D (\\u03c8_i, \\u03c7_1_i) JSD.\\n\\n### 3.4 Periodic handling\\n- All torsions wrapped to (\\u2212\\u03c0, \\u03c0] using `numpy.arctan2(sin, cos)` form.\\n- For TICA input only, each torsion is encoded as two features (sin \\u03b8, cos \\u03b8).\\n- For marginals and Ramachandran surfaces, raw angle values are used.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. TICA pipeline\\n\\n### 4.1 Inputs and lag\\n- Input features: `(sin, cos)`-encoded backbone \\u03c6, \\u03c8 (8 values), interior \\u03c9 (3 residues \\u00d7 2 = 6 values), and all defined \\u03c7 (count from \\u00a73.3 \\u00d7 2). Total feature dimension is panel-specific and reported in outputs.\\n- Lag \\u03c4 in **frames**. Default ladder {1, 2, 5, 10, 20}. \\u03c4 is selected per panel as follows:\\n  1. On the ground-truth-training frames, compute the integrated autocorrelation time \\u03c4_int of \\u03c8 of residue 1 as `1 + 2 \\u03a3_{k=1..K} C(k)` with C(k) the autocorrelation up to the first negative value (cut at K where C(K) \\u2264 0).\\n  2. Anchor candidate \\u03c4 values to fractions of \\u03c4_int: \\u03c4 \\u2208 {1, 2, 5, 10, 20} frames intersected with \\u230a\\u03c4_int / 4\\u230b, \\u230a\\u03c4_int / 2\\u230b, \\u230a\\u03c4_int\\u230b.\\n  3. Run the full TICA pipeline at each candidate \\u03c4. The primary \\u03c4 is the one whose **leading** implied timescale (see \\u00a74.3) is closest to but not exceeding \\u03c4_int (the slow-coordinates regime). Sensitivity (top-3 eigenvalues, JSD on top-2 IC coords, energy distance on top-4 ICs) is reported at every \\u03c4 in the ladder.\\n- Lag time \\u03c4 is reported in **frames** and in **time** using dt_save from \\u00a77.\\n\\n### 4.2 Number of components\\n- Compute eigenvalues \\u03bb_i of the tICA operator on the training split.\\n- Implied timescale per component: t_i = \\u2212 \\u03c4 / ln(\\u03bb_i) for \\u03bb_i \\u2208 (0, 1).\\n- Rank rule (binding):\\n  - k_cap = min(10, rank(X) where X is the cumulative-covariance matrix of the TICA input features \\u2014 i.e. \\u2264 number of non-zero-variance `(sin, cos)` tuples).\\n  - Include component i iff \\u03bb_i \\u2265 0.5 and t_i \\u2265 \\u2308\\u03c4 / \\u03c4_int_train\\u2309 \\u00d7 \\u03c4 (in frames) / reported_dt \\u2014 phrased in \\u00a74.3 as t_i \\u2265 \\u03c4_int_train (the autocorrelation time of the training split's fast variable).\\n  - Report the spectral gap (\\u0394 log \\u03bb_i \\u2212 \\u0394 log \\u03bb_{i+1} for the first 10 components) so the choice is auditable.\\n\\n### 4.3 Fit and project\\n- Implementation:\\n  - `pyemma.coordinates.tica(features_train, lag=tau, dim=k_cap, kinetic_map=True, mean=None)` \\u2014 `mean=None` triggers `featurize` mean computation on the training split itself, then propagated to all subsequent calls.\\n  - PyEMMA exposes `.transform(X)` for projection. Equivalent `deeptime.decomposition.TICA(lagtime=tau, dim=k_cap).fit(features_train).transform(features_test)` is documented as a swap-in alternative; the design picks PyEMMA and falls back to deeptime if PyEMMA is unavailable, with the resulting library recorded in `config.yaml`.\\n- Project verbatim: the saved `meanzero`, the `mean` vector, the sine/cosine basis, the lag \\u03c4, and the projection matrices are applied to ground-truth-evaluation-split frames **and** to forward-model frames with **no refit, no re-centering**.\\n\\n### 4.4 Outputs\\n- TICA scores `Z_train`, `Z_eval_MD`, `Z_Model` of shape `(n_frames, k_eff)` where k_eff is the number of components retained by \\u00a74.2.\\n- Eigenvalues \\u03bb_1..\\u03bb_k_cap and implied timescales t_1..t_k_cap on the training split.\\n- Spectral-gap table.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Metrics\\n\\n### 5.1 Per-CV metrics\\n\\n#### 5.1.1 1D marginals \\u2014 JSD bits and circular 1-Wasserstein\\nFor each torsion CV (\\u03c6_i, \\u03c8_i, \\u03c9_i, \\u03c7_j_i where defined) **and** each TICA score (top k_eff):\\n\\n- Discretize to a 50-bin uniform grid on (\\u2212\\u03c0, \\u03c0] for torsions; on the empirical [min, max] range *of the pooled ground-truth+model training union* of TICA scores (to keep bins fixed across the two distributions). For TICA scores we use min-max range with a 2% extender on each end to prevent empty edge cells.\\n- Bin masses p, q (probability mass per bin, summed to 1) with optional weights (per \\u00a72.4) and a small \\u03b5 = 1e-12 added per cell before normalization to avoid log 0.\\n- **Jensen\\u2013Shannon divergence (bits, natural log):**\\n  ```\\n  M = 0.5 * (p + q)\\n  JSD(p, q) = 0.5 * (KL(p || M) + KL(q || M)) / ln(2)\\n  ```\\n  using `scipy.stats.entropy(pk, qk=None, base=None)` then dividing by `ln(2)` to render in bits.\\n- **Circular 1-Wasserstein (radians):**\\n  - Duplicate each sample with a shift of \\u22122\\u03c0 and 2\\u03c0, compute the weighted 1D Wasserstein on the tripled support window [\\u2212\\u03c0 \\u2212 2\\u03c0, \\u03c0 + 2\\u03c0], and take the minimum transport cost; closed form available via `scipy.stats.wasserstein_1d(u_vals, v_vals, v_weights=u_weights)` after sorting. The reported value is the **circular W1 in radians** (it is bounded by \\u03c0).\\n\\n#### 5.1.2 2D surfaces\\nPer residue (\\u03c6_i, \\u03c8_i), per residue (\\u03c8_i, \\u03c7_1_i) where both are defined, and on (TICA-1, TICA-2):\\n\\n- 36 \\u00d7 36 grid; normalized counts (with optional weights per \\u00a72.4); \\u03b5 smoothing as above.\\n- JSD computed on the 1296-dimensional PMF via the same helper.\\n\\n#### 5.1.3 Multivariate (top-4 TICA components)\\n- Energy distance (Sz\\u00e9kely\\u2013Rizzo) on the four-dimensional TICA score vectors. Implemented as `scipy.spatial.distance.energy_distance(u, Z_train_eval, ..., weights=..., ...)` or with explicit U-statistic. The reported number is the energy distance (units of TICA scores) plus a bootstrap p-value against the null of equal distributions using the same test statistic.\\n\\n### 5.2 Per-frame-weighted autocorrelation times (informational only)\\nFor TICA-1..TICA-k_eff:\\n- \\u03c4_int = 1 + 2 \\u03a3_{k \\u2265 1} C(k), cut at the first non-positive C(k).\\n- C(k) is the per-frame-weighted TICA-score autocorrelation (sample-weighted, optional \\u00a72.4).\\n- Reported **per ensemble** (ground-truth eval split and forward-model) in the CSV row's `aux_*` columns. **Not** used in any pass/fail decision.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Statistical analysis plan\\n\\n### 6.1 Effect size and CI\\n- 95% CIs on JSD and W1 by replica-level block bootstrap:\\n  - Block length \\u2113_frames chosen per CV from the **training-split** autocorrelation time of that CV (so jittered coverage is in the units of \\\"blocks of training-typical correlation\\\").\\n  - B = 200 bootstrap replicates (illustrative default; logged in config). For each replicate:\\n    - Resample blocks with replacement within the **ground-truth eval split** \\u2192 p*; within the **forward-model set** \\u2192 q*; recompute JSD*(p*, q*) and W1*(p*, q*).\\n  - CI = 2.5 / 97.5 percentiles of the bootstrap distribution.\\n  - The same procedure applies for energy distance on top-4 ICs (with per-frame weighting only on the resampled frames).\\n\\n### 6.2 Family-wise error\\n- Family = per-CV rows aggregated into two pre-defined buckets:\\n  - Family A (1D torsion marginals): all defined \\u03c6_i, \\u03c8_i, \\u03c9_i, \\u03c7_j_i. |A| pre-computed per panel.\\n  - Family B (2D surfaces): Ramachandran per residue (4 rows), \\u03c8-vs-\\u03c71 per residue where defined, TICA-1\\u00d7TICA-2. |B| pre-computed per panel.\\n  - Family C (multivariate summaries): energy distance on top-4 TICA (one row).\\n- Benjamini\\u2013Hochberg FDR at q = 0.05 within each family using `statsmodels.stats.multitest.multipletests(pvals, alpha=0.05, method='fdr_bh')`. JSD-based p_value is the bootstrap p-value (two-sided) for the hypothesis JSD \\u2264 JSD_threshold per CV; default threshold = JSD ^ floor of bins/n_eff_actionability (see \\u00a76.3 power note). Family A and B use JSD-derived p-values; Family C uses the energy-distance p-value.\\n\\n### 6.3 n_eff and power\\n- Effective sample size n_eff per distribution = n_frames \\u00b7 dt_save / \\u03c4_int (training-split \\u03c4_int of the cached \\\"fast variable\\\" \\u03c8 residue 1).\\n- JSD precision floor \\u2248 bins / n_eff. Reported per CV in CSV `aux_*` columns: `(n_eff_MD, n_eff_Model, precision_floor_JSD_bits)`.\\n- The power note: with n_eff per side, the minimum detectable JSD (effect size) at power 0.8 for a BS distribution with B = 200 is reported as `JSD_detectable \\u2248 2 \\u00d7 precision_floor + 0.005 bits` (rule-of-thumb; logged for reproducibility).\\n\\n### 6.4 Seeds and determinism\\n- All randomness (block-bootstrap indices, KDE bandwiths if KDE is used in place of histograms) uses a single `SEED` integer from `config.yaml`. Default `SEED = 0xC0FFEE` (illustrative; replace per project).\\n- No per-set re-tuning of KDE bandwidths, bin edges, or tICA hyperparameters.\\n\\n### 6.5 Failure modes handled explicitly\\n- If both populations have all-zero variance on a CV (e.g. \\u03c9 staying near 180\\u00b0), JSD over (\\u03b5, 0, \\u03b5, \\u2026) is reported as NaN with reason `low_variance`.\\n- If a residue has no defined torsion (Gly / Pro / Ala), that CV row is omitted from family A pseudocode at run time and BH FDR proceeds over the remaining rows.\\n\\n### 6.6 Configuration schema (binding shape)\\n\\n```yaml\\n# config.yaml \\u2014 single panel; replicate key 'panels' for additional sequences\\npanel_id: \\\"ala4_ace_nme\\\"                      # illustrative name\\ntopology: \\\"/abs/path/to/ref.pdb\\\"\\nmd_trajectories:\\n  format: \\\"dcd\\\"                              # or xtc, nc, h5\\n  files:   [ \\\"/abs/path/md/replica_*.dcd\\\" ]   # exactly N replicas\\n  dt_save_fs: 100                            # used both for frame-to-time and tau\\n  t_equil_ns: 1.0                             # discarded at start per replica\\n  replicas: 100\\nforward_model:\\n  source: \\\"model://forward_v1\\\"\\n  trajectories:\\n    format: \\\"dcd\\\"\\n    files:   [ \\\"/abs/path/model/replica_*.dcd\\\" ]\\n  reported_T_K: 300\\n  reported_dt_save_fs: 100\\nsplitting:\\n  train_fraction: 0.7                         # by replica id\\n  seed: 12648430\\ntica:\\n  lag_ladder_frames: [1, 2, 5, 10, 20]\\n  selection_rule: \\\"first_tau_with_leading_timescale_le_tau_int_train\\\"\\n  library: \\\"pyemma\\\"                          # or \\\"deeptime\\\"\\n  max_components: 10\\n  kinetic_map: true\\nmetrics:\\n  bins_1d: 50\\n  bins_2d: 36\\n  epsilon: 1.0e-12\\nbootstrap:\\n  n_replicates: 200\\n  block_strategy: \\\"from_tau_int_train_per_cv\\\"\\n  seed: 12648430\\nfdr:\\n  q: 0.05\\n  family_definition:\\n    A: \\\"torsions_1d\\\"\\n    B: \\\"surfaces_2d\\\"\\n    C: \\\"energy_distance_top4\\\"\\noutput:\\n  csv: \\\"/abs/path/results/per_cv.csv\\\"\\n  figure_dir: \\\"/abs/path/figures/\\\"\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Controls\\n\\n| Control | Description | Verified by |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `T` matched | Ground-truth reported temperature = forward-model reported temperature | unit test on `config.yaml` + assertion `md.reported_T_K == forward_model.reported_T_K` |\\n| `dt_save` matched | Frame-to-time conversion same on both sides | `assert md.dt_save_fs == forward_model.reported_dt_save_fs` |\\n| `t_equil` matched | Equilibration prefix discarded on both sides | log printed per panel; if forward model does not declare t_equil, design flags `no_t_equil_declared: true` and reports results both with and without discarding the first 10% of frames as a sensitivity row |\\n| `termini capping, protonation, force field, water treatment` matched | Topology source = single source of truth; topology file used by featurize is the one shipped with the reference MD run; topology audit table records every substitution atom in `config.yaml`'s `termini` block | diff at run start: `featurize(MD_frame_0) == featurize(Model_frame_0)` upto index ordering |\\n| identical featurization function | One `featurize()` implementation is reused for both ensembles; no per-source code path | grep for `featurize(` call sites limited to `featurize/` module |\\n| no per-set KDE bandwidth | Either histogram-only (bins fixed by \\u00a76) or KDE bandwidth fixed to a single Silverman value computed on the pooled training-split TICA scores | unit test asserts no per-set bandwidth override |\\n| fixed integer seeds | All randomness uses `SEED` from `config.yaml`; no `np.random.seed` inside loops | grep + assertion |\\n| no TICA refit on test data | Fit happens once on training split; `.transform()` only calls | assertion that `tica.fit` is called at most once and `tica.transform` does not mutate state |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compute & feasibility\\n\\n### 8.1 Environment\\n- The current bubblewrap at `/workspace` contains only `/workspace/task.json`. `python3` is present at `/usr/bin/python3`. Numeric packages (NumPy, SciPy, pandas, mdtraj, PyEMMA, deeptime, scikit-learn, matplotlib) are **not** importable. The pip executable is not on `PATH`. Network access is disabled. Consequently **no Python execution of the analysis is performed here**; this document is the deliverable.\\n- The design assumes the eventual runtime is a CPU-only Linux with the following versions (illustrative; logged in `requirements.txt`):\\n  - Python 3.10+\\n  - `mdtraj >= 1.9`, `pyemma >= 2.5` (or `deeptime >= 0.4`), `scipy >= 1.10`, `scikit-learn >= 1.2`, `numpy >= 1.23`, `pandas >= 1.5`, `matplotlib >= 3.6`.\\n\\n### 8.2 Compute sizing (binding)\\n- Featurization (`compute_phi/psi/omega/chi1..n` \\u00d7 4 residues, max ~100k frames per replica, ~100 replicas): on 24 CPUs, low single-digit minutes.\\n- Mean + covariance + lagged covariance + eigendecomposition for the tICA input features: tens of seconds per \\u03c4 on the chosen training split.\\n- KDE / 1D and 2D histograms: seconds per CV.\\n- Block bootstrap (B = 200) over the eval split on the largest CV family: low single-digit minutes; dominates wall-clock.\\n- Total wall-clock for a single panel: **under one hour of CPU time** at the illustrative panel size. Memory peak \\u2264 ~2 GB numpy arrays for the largest expected training split (illustrative).\\n\\n### 8.3 Network and install policy inside the bubble\\n- The protocol presupposes `pip install` for the libraries above. This is **not** available in the present bubble. The Markdown design is therefore shipped as the final deliverable; no execution artifact is produced.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Limitations (stated explicitly)\\n\\n- The design is single-panel by default. Multi-panel execution reuses all of \\u00a73\\u2013\\u00a76 without code changes but multiplies compute; CI values are computed **per panel** and not pooled.\\n- \\\"Distributional similarity\\\" is the equilibrium p(x) picture. Kinetics are **not** graded for pass/fail; the autocorrelation row is informational.\\n- If the forward model does not declare weights, weights are uniformly 1.\\n- If MD and model use different integration `dt`, the protocol does not rescale; we record `dt_save` as a control and report sensitivity only if the mismatch is small (a separate standalone check described in \\u00a76.6; not used for pass/fail).\\n- JSD floors at 0 and W1 floors at 0 radians; both are documented lower bounds that depend on KDE/histogram resolution. Power floor is reported per CV.\\n- The reference backbone-dihedral computation in mdtraj assigns \\u03c6_1 and \\u03c8_4 by using cap atoms; this convention is non-universal and is recorded per panel. Re-running with a different cap convention will change \\u03c8_4 / \\u03c6_1 by an atom-shift of one bond, which can slightly alter JSD on those two CVs only.\\n- If MD and model samples are tiny (n_frames per replica < 100), bootstrap variance dominates; the design flags `n_eff_low: true` and declines to compute BH FDR on Family C (energy distance) only \\u2014 Families A and B continue with the appropriate JSD floor caveat.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Inline deliverables\\n\\n### 10.1 Per-CV CSV row schema (binding)\\n\\n```\\nCV_name, ensemble_size, n_eff,\\nJSD_mean, JSD_CI_lo, JSD_CI_hi,\\nW1_mean, W1_CI_lo, W1_CI_hi,\\np_value_MMD_if_applicable,\\naux_var_MD, aux_var_Model, aux_tau_int_MD, aux_tau_int_Model,\\nfamily_tag,\\npanel_id\\n```\\n\\n| Field | Type | Description |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `CV_name` | str | e.g. `phi_2`, `psi_1`, `omega_2`, `chi1_3`, `TICA_1`, `ram_2`, `psichi1_3`, `tica12` |\\n| `ensemble_size` | int | number of frames in the eval split (smaller of MD eval vs. Model total) |\\n| `n_eff` | float | min(n_eff_MD, n_eff_Model) per \\u00a76.3 |\\n| `JSD_mean` | float (bits) | point estimate JSD on full eval splits |\\n| `JSD_CI_lo / _hi` | float | 2.5 / 97.5 percentile of B block-bootstrap replicates |\\n| `W1_mean`, `W1_CI_lo`, `W1_CI_hi` | float (radians for torsions, TICA-units otherwise) | circular W1 on torsions (else linear W1 over the bin range) |\\n| `p_value_MMD_if_applicable` | float or null | energy-distance bootstrap p-value for Family C only; null for Families A, B |\\n| `aux_var_MD`, `aux_var_Model` | float | sample variances of the CV after per-CV discretization |\\n| `aux_tau_int_MD`, `aux_tau_int_Model` | float (frames) | per-frame-weighted \\u03c4_int of the CV (informational) |\\n| `family_tag` | enum | `A_torsions_1d`, `B_surfaces_2d`, `C_energy_distance` |\\n| `panel_id` | str | mirrors `config.yaml`'s top-level key |\\n\\n### 10.2 Figure manifests (binding)\\n\\nEach figure is a PNG written to `output.figure_dir/<panel_id>/<figure_name>.png` at 150 dpi.\\n\\n| ID | Filename | Description | Type | Library |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| F1 | `rama_<resi>.png` | Ramachandran (\\u03c6, \\u03c8) per residue; ground-truth and model contours overlaid; free-energy levels at 1, 2, 4, 8 kT | 2D contour overlay | matplotlib |\\n| F2 | `psi_chi1_<resi>.png` | 2D (\\u03c8, \\u03c7_1) per residue where \\u03c7_1 defined | 2D contour overlay | matplotlib |\\n| F3 | `tica12_overlay.png` | 2D (TICA-1, TICA-2) free-energy overlay, ground truth vs. model | 2D contour overlay | matplotlib |\\n| F4 | `tica_scree_<panel>.png` | tICA eigenvalues and implied timescales vs. component index | 1D line + twin axis | matplotlib |\\n| F5 | `tau_sensitivity_<panel>.png` | Top-3 metrics (leading eigenvalue, JSD on TICA-1/2, energy distance on top-4 ICs) vs. \\u03c4 in lag ladder | 1D line | matplotlib |\\n| F6 | `cv_jitter_<panel>.png` | Mean JSD per CV family with 95% bars | dot-and-whisker | matplotlib |\\n| F7 | `autocorr_times_<panel>.png` | \\u03c4_int per TICA component on MD and Model (informational) | 1D line | matplotlib |\\n\\n### 10.3 Pseudocode (binding)\\n\\nAll pseudocode in fenced blocks below is descriptive; no execution is undertaken.\\n\\n#### 10.3.1 `featurize`\\n\\n```python\\n# featurize/torsions.py\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport mdtraj as md\\n\\ndef residues_with_chi(topo, j):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return list of residue indices whose sidechain exposes chi_j.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    # For j=1 use md.compute_chi1 indices; skip residues with NaN definition.\\n    return [r.index for r in topo.residues if has_chi(r, j)]\\n\\ndef featurize(traj: md.Trajectory) -> dict:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Compute, for a single Trajectory, the per-residue torsion arrays\\n    plus their (sin, cos) encodings.\\n    Returns a dict with keys: 'phi' (n,4), 'psi' (n,4), 'omega' (n,3),\\n    'chi_j' for each defined j, 'chi_indices', and the sin/cos blocks.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    n = traj.n_frames\\n    out = {\\n        'phi': md.compute_phi(traj)[1],                 # shape (n, 4)\\n        'psi': md.compute_psi(traj)[1],                 # shape (n, 4)\\n        'omega': md.compute_omega(traj)[1],             # shape (n, 3)\\n        'chi_indices': {}\\n    }\\n    for j in range(1, 5):\\n        idx = residues_with_chi(traj.topology, j)\\n        if not idx:\\n            continue\\n        chi_vals, chooser = md.compute_chi(j)(traj)     # shape (n, len(idx))\\n        out[f'chi{j}'] = chi_vals\\n        out['chi_indices'][f'chi{j}'] = idx\\n\\n    # Periodic encoding for TICA input only.\\n    def enc(arr):\\n        # Wrap to (-pi, pi] defensively (mdtraj already does this).\\n        return np.stack([np.sin(arr), np.cos(arr)], axis=-1)  # (n, k, 2)\\n    tica_blocks = []\\n    for key in ['phi', 'psi', 'omega']:\\n        tica_blocks.append(enc(out[key]).reshape(n, -1))      # (n, k*2)\\n    for j in range(1, 5):\\n        if f'chi{j}' in out:\\n            tica_blocks.append(enc(out[f'chi{j}']).reshape(n, -1))\\n    out['tica_input'] = np.concatenate(tica_blocks, axis=1)\\n    return out\\n```\\n\\n#### 10.3.2 `fit_tica`\\n\\n```python\\n# tica/fit.py\\nimport numpy as np, pyemma.coordinates as pc\\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.stattools import acf\\n# (deeptime alternative path documented but not executed in pseudocode)\\n\\ndef estimate_tau_int_train(features_train_1d, dt_save_fs):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Integrated autocorrelation time of psi_1 (uses column index from\\n    earlier featurize call). Cuts at first non-positive autocorrelation.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    series = features_train_1d[:, psi1_column]\\n    c = acf(series, nlags=len(series)//2, fft=True)\\n    tau = 1.0\\n    for k in range(1, len(c)):\\n        if c[k] <= 0:\\n            break\\n        tau += 2.0 * c[k]\\n    return tau\\n\\ndef fit_tica(traj_md_train, tau, k_cap):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"One fit, on the ground-truth training split only.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    feats = featurize(traj_md_train)['tica_input']      # (n, d)\\n    tica = pc.tica(data=feats, lag=tau, dim=k_cap,\\n                   kinetic_map=True, mean=None)         # mean=None -> learned\\n    return tica\\n\\ndef project(tica, feats):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Apply the fitted tICA operator verbatim.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return tica.transform(feats)\\n```\\n\\n#### 10.3.3 `per_cv_metric`\\n\\n```python\\n# metrics/per_cv.py\\nimport numpy as np\\nfrom scipy.stats import entropy, wasserstein_1d\\n\\ndef jsd_bits(p, q, eps=1e-12):\\n    p = (np.asarray(p) + eps); q = (np.asarray(q) + eps)\\n    p = p / p.sum(); q = q / q.sum()\\n    m = 0.5 * (p + q)\\n    return (entropy(p, m, base=None) + entropy(q, m, base=None)) / np.log(2)\\n\\ndef circular_wasserstein_1d(u_vals, v_vals, u_weights=None, v_weights=None):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Circular 1-Wasserstein via triplication on [-3pi, 3pi]. Returns radians.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    def shift(arr, w, delta):\\n        return np.concatenate([arr + delta for _ in (1,)])  # placeholder\\n    # Pseudocode; concrete impl: triple concatenate with shifts -2pi, 0, +2pi,\\n    # run weighted wasserstein_1d on the doubled supports, return minimum / 2.\\n    triples_u = np.concatenate([u_vals - 2*np.pi, u_vals, u_vals + 2*np.pi])\\n    triples_v = np.concatenate([v_vals - 2*np.pi, v_vals, v_vals + 2*np.pi])\\n    wU = np.tile(u_weights, 3) if u_weights is not None else None\\n    wV = np.tile(v_weights, 3) if v_weights is not None else None\\n    return wasserstein_1d(triples_u, triples_v, v_weights=wU, u_weights=wV) / 3.0\\n\\ndef per_cv_metric(p_hist, q_hist, edges, two_d=False):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Args: 1D or 2D PMFs over the same fixed grid; returns dict.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return {\\n        'JSD_mean': jsd_bits(p_hist, q_hist),\\n        # For 1D torsion CVs, also compute circular W1:\\n        #   W1 = circular_wasserstein_1d(samples_md, samples_model, ...)\\n        # and store under W1_mean.\\n        # W1 reported only for 1D torsion rows, so two_d=False branch.\\n    }\\n```\\n\\n#### 10.3.4 Block bootstrap\\n\\n```python\\n# stats/bootstrap.py\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\ndef block_length_from_tau_int(series, dt_save_fs):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Block length in frames for replica-level bootstrap on a single CV.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    c = autocorrelation(series)\\n    tau = 1 + 2 * c_positive_sum(c)\\n    return max(1, int(np.ceil(tau)))\\n\\ndef block_bootstrap(md_eval_features_by_replica,\\n                    model_features_by_replica,\\n                    metric_fn, block_len_md, block_len_model,\\n                    B=200, seed=12648430):\\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\\n    boot = []\\n    for _ in range(B):\\n        md_blocks = sample_blocks(md_eval_features_by_replica, block_len_md, rng)\\n        mod_blocks = sample_blocks(model_features_by_replica, block_len_model, rng)\\n        boot.append(metric_fn(np.concatenate(md_blocks),\\n                              np.concatenate(mod_blocks)))\\n    return np.percentile(boot, [2.5, 97.5]), boot\\n```\\n\\n#### 10.3.5 FDR application\\n\\n```python\\n# stats/fdr.py\\nfrom statsmodels.stats.multitest import multipletests\\n\\ndef decide_families(per_cv_rows, q=0.05):\\n    out = {}\\n    for fam in ['A_torsions_1d', 'B_surfaces_2d', 'C_energy_distance']:\\n        rows = [r for r in per_cv_rows if r['family_tag'] == fam]\\n        if fam == 'C_energy_distance':\\n            pvals = [r['p_value_MMD_if_applicable'] for r in rows]\\n        else:\\n            pvals = [2 * (1 - cdf_of_jsd(r)) for r in rows]\\n        reject, p_adj, _, _ = multipletests(pvals, alpha=q, method='fdr_bh')\\n        for r, rej, pa in zip(rows, reject, p_adj):\\n            r['reject_null'] = bool(rej)\\n            r['p_adj'] = float(pa)\\n        out[fam] = rows\\n    return out\\n```\\n\\n#### 10.3.6 CSV emission\\n\\n```python\\n# io/csv_emitter.py\\nimport csv\\n\\nFIELDNAMES = [\\n    'CV_name', 'ensemble_size', 'n_eff',\\n    'JSD_mean', 'JSD_CI_lo', 'JSD_CI_hi',\\n    'W1_mean', 'W1_CI_lo', 'W1_CI_hi',\\n    'p_value_MMD_if_applicable',\\n    'aux_var_MD', 'aux_var_Model',\\n    'aux_tau_int_MD', 'aux_tau_int_Model',\\n    'family_tag', 'panel_id'\\n]\\n\\ndef emit(rows, path):\\n    with open(path, 'w', newline='') as f:\\n        w = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=FIELDNAMES)\\n        w.writeheader()\\n        for r in rows:\\n            w.writerow({k: r.get(k, '') for k in FIELDNAMES})\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Illustrative output (placeholders only \\u2014 no run performed)\\n\\nA representative row of `per_cv.csv` after running on the worked-example panel (numbers are placeholders):\\n\\n```\\nCV_name,ensemble_size,n_eff,JSD_mean,JSD_CI_lo,JSD_CI_hi,W1_mean,W1_CI_lo,W1_CI_hi,p_value_MMD_if_applicable,aux_var_MD,aux_var_Model,aux_tau_int_MD,aux_tau_int_Model,family_tag,panel_id\\nphi_2,210000,7943.5,0.0031,0.0028,0.0035,0.041,0.038,0.044,,0.91,0.90,2.4,2.5,A_torsions_1d,ala4_ace_nme\\npsi_2,210000,7860.1,0.0029,0.0026,0.0033,0.038,0.036,0.041,,0.88,0.88,2.5,2.6,A_torsions_1d,ala4_ace_nme\\nomega_2,210000,8200.0,0.0012,0.0010,0.0014,,      ,,0.05,0.05,3.0,3.1,A_torsions_1d,ala4_ace_nme\\nchi1_3,210000,7000.0,0.0201,0.0185,0.0221,0.151,0.143,0.161,,1.10,1.21,3.4,3.8,A_torsions_1d,ala4_ace_nme\\nram_2,210000,7943.5,0.0064,0.0058,0.0070,,,,,0.85,0.83,,,B_surfaces_2d,ala4_ace_nme\\npsichi1_3,210000,7000.0,0.0312,0.0294,0.0334,,,,,0.92,0.95,,,B_surfaces_2d,ala4_ace_nme\\ntica12,210000,7943.5,0.0044,0.0040,0.0048,,,,,1.05,1.05,,,B_surfaces_2d,ala4_ace_nme\\nplanar_top4,210000,7943.5,,,, ,,,,1.0,0.012,0.005,0.022,,,,,C_energy_distance,ala4_ace_nme\\n```\\n*(all numeric values above are illustrative placeholders)*\\n\\nA representative set of 7 figure files (paths under `output.figure_dir/ala4_ace_nme/`):\\n- `rama_1.png`, `rama_2.png`, `rama_3.png`, `rama_4.png`\\n- `psi_chi1_2.png`, `psi_chi1_3.png` (only residues where \\u03c7_1 is defined)\\n- `tica12_overlay.png`\\n- `tica_scree.png`\\n- `tau_sensitivity.png`\\n- `cv_jitter.png`\\n- `autocorr_times.png`\\n\\nA representative FDR outcome (illustrative):\\n- Family A (`A_torsions_1d`): per-CV JSD-derived p-values, BH-adjusted; in this placeholder run, all rows show `reject_null = False` (preserved_null) due to small effect sizes with tight CIs, except `chi1_3` which is flagged preserved_null but with JSD_mean = 0.020 (above the bins/n_eff floor of 0.0071) \\u2014 represented here as informational, not as a pass/fail statement.\\n- Family B (`B_surfaces_2d`): analogous.\\n- Family C (`C_energy_distance`): `planar_top4` energy-distance bootstrap p-value placeholder = 0.012; BH-adjusted at the family-level (single row test) preserves rejection control at q = 0.05.\\n\\n*(All values illustrative; not from any actual run.)*\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Stated assumptions and open clarifications \\u2014 resolved status\\n\\nThe original plan's open items 1\\u201312 are **resolved by the binding defaults** above:\\n1. Sequence/scope \\u2192 single canonical panel, multi-panel-ready (\\u00a76.6).\\n2. Endpoint \\u2192 equilibrium p(x); kinetic is informational only (\\u00a71, \\u00a75.2).\\n3. Weights \\u2192 unweighted default; rules in \\u00a72.4 if present.\\n4. Library stack \\u2192 \\u00a78.1 records it; \\u00a710.3 pseudocode targets it.\\n5. Lag & k \\u2192 \\u00a74.1 ladder + \\u00a74.2 spectral-gap rule.\\n6. Featurization caps \\u2192 \\u00a73.2 bound to cap substitution; recorded in config.\\n7. JSD bits \\u2192 \\u00a75.1.1.\\n8. Block-bootstrap \\u00b7 FDR \\u00b7 n_eff \\u00b7 power note \\u2192 \\u00a76.1\\u2013\\u00a76.3.\\n9. Controls \\u2192 \\u00a77 table binding.\\n10. Deliverable format \\u2192 \\u00a710.\\n11. \\u03b5 smoothing \\u2192 \\u00a75.1.1 (`epsilon = 1e-12`).\\n12. Informational kinematic check (no pass/fail) \\u2192 \\u00a75.2.\\n\\nItems that are still upstream of the design (i.e. data not in the repo) are:\\n- exact identity of the canonical tetrapeptide, force field, water treatment;\\n- exact format / path of MD and forward-model trajectories;\\n- exact vers of mdtraj / PyEMMA / deeptime / scipy / scikit-learn / matplotlib;\\n- whether a future revision wants temperature-rescaling reweighting.\\n\\nThese are intentionally left to the project owner; the protocol is unchanged by the answers and \\u00a76.6 captures their pinned values.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**End of design.** Document is the deliverable; no execution was performed.\\n\", \"evidence\": \"Initial inspecti